"explanation": "The primary sources of dissolved salts in the ocean are the weathering and erosion of continental rocks (which releases ions like sodium and chloride into rivers), volcanic outgassing (which releases gases that dissolve and react in seawater), and hydrothermal reactions on the sea floor (which leach minerals from oceanic crust). Melting glacial ice, however, consists of fresh water (precipitation that has been filtered and frozen). While it may contain trace impurities, it does not add significant salt to the ocean; in fact, it dilutes the salinity of the surrounding seawater. Therefore, melting glacial ice is the exception among the listed sources of ocean salt.",
